--TEST--
A `spawn script` child runs under the capability grant in force where it was spawned: it inherits the narrowing its parent's `[[app]]` block made, and neither an `[[app]]` block naming the child nor a `Core\Config::set` widens it back — ADR 0006 § *Decision*, `rule:config/every-matching-app-block-applies-least-specific-first`, `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`: `spawn script` goes through a door that asks for
# `script.spawn` first, and deny-by-default means a case with no configuration
# cannot spawn at all. Granted unscoped so that the only capability this case
# measures is `fs.read`.
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

# The host's grant, over this whole directory. `secret.txt` is inside it, and
# the block below is what takes it away again — so a refusal further down is
# the narrowing's and not the host's.
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]

# `rule:config/every-matching-app-block-applies-least-specific-first`: the block keyed on the entry file being run. A value array
# replaces where a `[[table]]` appends, so `fs.read` in force for this run is
# `./data` alone. This is the narrowing everything below is measured against.
[[app]]
entry = "case.nvs"

[app.capabilities.fs]
read = ["./data"]

# The block keyed on the child, asking for the host's whole grant back. It
# never takes effect: `Ctx::isolate` hands a child the configuration in force
# where it was spawned rather than re-resolving the tree for the child's own
# entry file, and that field's own doc owns why — a child re-reading the
# snapshot would silently widen back every narrowing its parent made.
[[app]]
entry = "child.nvs"

[app.capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--FILE data/note.txt--
note
--FILE secret.txt--
secret
--FILE child.nvs--
<?nvs
// Inherited, not granted: nothing in the child's own configuration is what
// makes this read succeed, because the child was handed the parent's snapshot.
echo "child read ", Core\IO::read('./data/note.txt');

// Outside the parent's grant, inside the host's, and named by an `[[app]]`
// block of the child's own — the widening, asked for in the one place a
// configuration could still ask for it. Refused, and refused as `rule:security/denial-is-a-runtime-error`'s
// catchable `RuntimeError` naming the capability rather than as a `FATAL`.
try {
    echo Core\IO::read('./secret.txt');
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "child: ", $denied->message, "\n";
}

// The other way to ask. `[capabilities]` is a `RuntimeTighten` directive and a
// grant is a list rather than a quantity, so `set` cannot narrow it and will
// not widen it: `crates/nvs-config/src/request.rs`'s module doc owns why
// refusing outright is the safe direction. `false`, never a throw.
echo "child set = ", Core\Config::set('capabilities.fs.read', '.') ? "true" : "false", "\n";

// And the refusal left the grant exactly where it was.
try {
    Core\IO::read('./secret.txt');
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $still) {
    echo "child still refused", "\n";
}
--FILE--
<?nvs
// M6's *Verify*: a child cannot widen a capability its parent narrowed. The
// parent's own reading first, so that what the child prints is a comparison
// against a known grant: `./data` reads, `./secret.txt` does not.
echo "parent read ", Core\IO::read('./data/note.txt');
try {
    Core\IO::read('./secret.txt');
    echo "unreachable", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    echo "parent refused", "\n";
}

// `output: 'capture'` for the reason a-child-shares-nothing-with-its-parent
// gives: the child's lines arrive here as a value the parent prints, so their
// order against the parent's own is a fact rather than a race.
var $job = spawn script "child.nvs" with(output: "capture");
var $done = await $job;
echo "ok=", $done->ok ? "true" : "false", "\n";
echo $done->output;
--EXPECTF--
parent read note
parent refused
ok=true
child read note
child: Core\IO::read needs the capability `fs.read` for %s%esecret.txt, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`
child set = false
child still refused
